abstract = {Against the backdrop of rapid development in Internet of Things (IoT) and military intelligence, traditional cloud computing models are increasingly revealing their limitations when addressing geographically dispersed deployments, high real-time requirements, and stringent security needs. These challenges become even more pronounced in battlefield environments, where centralized data processing leads to issues such as network latency, bandwidth pressure, and energy consumption. Edge computing effectively enhances response speed, security, and robustness by decentralizing computing, storage, and analytical capabilities to edge nodes such as tactical terminals, drones, and vehicle-mounted devices close to data sources, providing critical technical support for modern warfare. This study examines the three-tier architecture of edge computing and analyzes its core technologies, including lightweight models, federated learning, and low-latency communication. Practical implications are explored through U.S. military applications, such as F-35 edge electronic warfare systems and the Cyber Command's "Persistent Network Hunting" program, demonstrating edge computing's value in tactical coordination, situational awareness, and electronic warfare. Furthermore, this study identifies current challenges and explores future development trajectories of edge computing. These contributions provide a theoretical foundation and technical insights for deploying distributed intelligent systems in military IoT networks.},
